    {
      "id": 19,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Reading this dashboard",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 11,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 46
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**Source.** Kyverno controller metrics (`kyverno_*`) from the four controller metrics services, plus container metrics from cAdvisor and kube-state-metrics. Names and labels checked against Kyverno v1.19.1.\n\n- Policy results count rule evaluations, not distinct resources. A background scan re-counts the same failing resource every cycle, and admission results include server-side dry runs (`dry_run=\"true\"`): Flux dry-run-applies every object it manages on each reconcile (every 10 minutes for taskflow), so an unchanged object that breaks a rule is counted again on every reconcile as well. Dry runs are kept on purpose: when an Enforce rule blocks a Flux change, the dry run is the only evaluation Kyverno records.\n- Label exposure is set in the HelmRelease (`metricsConfig.metricsExposure`, keyed by Kyverno's instrument names, which have no `_total`; the chart's own `_total` keys never matched). Once the controllers have been restarted after that change (Kyverno reads it at start-up), `kyverno_policy_results_total` has neither `resource_namespace` nor `policy_namespace`, and `kyverno_admission_requests_total` has no `resource_namespace`; series from before the restart keep the labels in history. Rule info drops `policy_namespace` (it never has `resource_namespace`). The execution-duration histogram drops `resource_namespace` and `resource_request_operation`; the admission-review histogram drops `resource_namespace` (it never has `policy_namespace`).\n- Rule info and policy changes are exported by both the admission and background controllers; the panels count each once.\n- The controllers have no CPU limit, so CPU is shown against the request.\n- Current violations per resource are in Policy Reporter (https://kyverno.jokelab.dev); this dashboard shows evaluation activity and controller health.\n- Counters appear after their first event, so empty panels on a quiet cluster are normal. Counts use increase_pure() for series born inside the range (a restart starts new series) and increase() for series that already existed before it.\n- Admission latency is only the synchronous part of the webhook. Audit-mode policies run after the response, so with Audit-only policies it shows webhook overhead, not rule cost. Latency histograms cannot resolve anything below their smallest 5 ms bucket."
      }
    }
  ],
  "graphTooltip": 1
